From: Dave Seddon <dave.seddon.ca@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 6 Jul 2026 09:34:08 -0700
Subject: [RFC net-next v1 0/1] net: flow_dissector: adaptive auto-enable for the fast-path gates
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

This RFC applies on top of "[PATCH net-next] net: flow_dissector: opt-in
byte-identical fast paths + tunnel inner descent" and is posted
separately because, unlike that series, it is a policy question rather
than a merge request.

The base series adds per-shape counters (/proc/net/flow_dissector_stats)
and a measured cost model: enabling a byte-identical shape gate is
net-positive exactly when the shape's matching fraction exceeds its
break-even p_be = C / (S + C), where S is the per-match saving and C the
hot-path miss cost -- both measured per shape per uarch. That makes "is
this gate worth turning on?" a derived number rather than a guess.

This patch turns that into an in-kernel "auto" mode so the operator sets
one knob instead of many. Decisions are driven by a packet-count window
(net.flow_dissector.auto_window_packets), not a wall-clock tick: every N
dissects the kernel compares each byte-identical shape's windowed
eligible fraction to its break-even and flips the gate, with hysteresis,
a multi-window dwell, and a flip-rate cap. It is traffic-proportional (a
busy link adapts in milliseconds, an idle link does not churn); the hot
path only samples, and the static_branch flips run in a workqueue.

MPLS is not auto-managed (its saving barely exceeds the miss cost on
in-order cores). The tunnel inner-descent gates from the base series are
never auto-managed: they change hashing, so enabling them must stay a
deliberate operator action.

The open question for the list: where should this loop live? The same
policy can run in a userspace agent (read the stats file, write the
sysctls, identical thresholds), so this is "single in-kernel knob" vs
"mechanism not policy + a reference agent". The counters in the base
series are the common foundation for either.

Supporting data (per-shape/per-arch break-even derivation):
https://github.com/randomizedcoder/xdp2 , under perf-results/ .

Dave Seddon (1):
